  "why": "The statement names 'the implicit graph conjecture', which in the literature (Kannan-Naor-Rudich, Spinrad, the linked article, Dujmovic et al.) is the factorial-speed statement; Hatami and Hatami refuted it in full in November 2021 (FOCS 2022 acceptance counts as digestion), with a non-constructive counterexample family, so that is the whole event, before the snapshot. The 'small' (n!·c^n) Small Implicit Graph Conjecture posed by Bonnet et al. in September 2024 is a strengthened-hypothesis variant of an already-refuted conjecture, not the statement's question, so its partial results are variant. Dujmovic et al. 2026 refines the constant to (1+o(1)) log n for proper minor-closed classes, which a positive case of a refuted universal claim cannot advance; scored variant (optimal-constant question). Other neighbours (PUG conjecture, randomized communication, Alon's dense regime, faithful universal graphs, geometric labelling) are different questions. No AI involvement anywhere.",
  "doubts": "The close call is the reading of 'slowly-growing': under the n!·c^n reading the problem would be open with in-window partial progress (bonnet-small-2024 as bound/case, dujmovic-2026 as case). I chose the factorial reading because the statement names the conjecture by its established name and the refutation predates the list. The frozen January 2024 wording was not retrieved. FOCS 2022 acceptance rests on snippets.",
